<TopicPage slug="subjects/literature/intermediate/shakespeares-craft">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Shakespeare (1564–1616) is literature's central figure for a craft reason, not a
    patriotic one: <strong>he expanded what dramatic language could hold</strong>. Verse and
    prose switched like gears for meaning, metaphor compressed to puns, characters built
    from contradictions rather than resolved into types — and four centuries of re-staging
    from Tokyo opera to Bollywood have not exhausted the machines. You met his verse
    mechanics in the poetry lesson; here we work them inside drama, where they do
    storytelling, characterisation and comedy all at once.
  </p>
  <p>
    The Paper 2 unit frames him against four very different set texts — Priestley's
    An Inspector Calls, Wilde's The Importance of Being Earnest, Pearl Cleage's Blues for
    an Alabama Sky, Chinonyerem Odimba's Princess &amp; The Hustler — with The Tempest as
    the Shakespeare option. The comparison is the point: every one of those plays lives or
    dies by what the words do in a mouth, and Shakespeare simply pushed that fact further
    than anyone before or since.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the verse system, the word-hoard, the contradictions, the afterlife" widget="equationStep" config={shakesCfg} />

  <h2>Drama as a designed machine</h2>
  <p>
    Start with the form. A play is a text designed to disappear into performance: its
    "meaning" is completed by bodies, stages and audiences. Shakespeare's plays come with
    almost no stage directions — no lighting cues, no "he hesitates" — because the
    <em>verse itself</em> carries those instructions. This is why close reading matters
    double in Shakespeare: the meter tells the actor where to pause, the pronouns tell the
    actor how the relationship stands, and the genre of the sentence (declaration, question,
    fragment) is the blocking. Reading him properly means reading the script the way an
    actor does: as a score.
  </p>
  <p>
    The playhouse itself explains some of the writing. The Globe (built 1599) was a
    daylight, open-air amphitheatre holding roughly three thousand spectators — groundlings
    standing for a penny, gentry in the galleries — which is why the plays braid lyrical
    verse for the connoisseurs with bawdy jokes and swordfights for the yard, and why
    storms, ghosts and battles are narrated in words rather than built with effects: the
    language had to do the scenery. Women's parts were played by boys, so heroines get
    some of the longest, most self-aware speeches in the canon — Rosalind, Viola and
    Portia all spend their plays inside a disguise the casting made literal. When you
    read, remember the conditions: every speech was engineered for one theatre, one
    company of actors, and daylight.
  </p>
  <p>
    It also means the exam skill is doubled vision: what the line says on the page, and
    what it does when spoken to another character in front of an audience. A soliloquy is
    neither — a character alone, thinking aloud, the audience turned confidant. Tracking
    which mode a speech is in (dialogue, aside, soliloquy) tells you who the words are
    actually for, and much of the irony lives in that gap.
  </p>

  <h2>The verse craft: a working gear system</h2>
  <p>
    The base line is iambic pentameter — five da-DUM feet, the rhythm of English thought at
    full stretch. Shakespeare's genius is the switches. <strong>Verse versus prose</strong>:
    nobles and tragic heroes speak verse; clowns, drunks and servants speak prose; and when
    a noble character drops into prose, something has broken — Hamlet "antic-disposed",
    Lady Macbeth sleepwalking in broken prose because her composed verse is gone.
    <strong>Shared lines</strong>: one pentameter split between two speakers means the
    second cannot wait — when Macbeth and Lady Macbeth split a line around the murder
    ("My hands are of your colour, but I shame / To wear a heart so white"), the meter
    enacts complicity: two minds finishing one thought. <strong>Rhymed couplets</strong>:
    the click at a scene's end, closing it like a door. <strong>Short lines</strong>: a
    pentameter with missing beats leaves a silence the actor must fill — grief, shock, or
    a lie being chosen.
  </p>
  <p>
    A worked case from the set text. The Tempest's opening storm is done entirely in
    shouting prose and broken lines — the boatswain's "Blow till thou burst thy wind, if
    room enough!" — while Prospero, backstage, calmly explains in verse that the storm is
    his. Form is information: chaos below decks, control above. Caliban, the "savage
    slave", speaks some of the most beautiful verse in the play — "Be not afeard; the isle
    is full of noises" — while the drunken butler Stephano speaks prose. Shakespeare's
    registers cut against his characters' social labels on purpose, and noticing where a
    speech sits on the verse-prose dial is often the whole answer.
  </p>

  <h2>Disguise, irony and the second audience</h2>
  <p>
    Renaissance drama loved disguise because it manufactures <strong>dramatic irony</strong>
    cheaply and permanently: the audience knows what half the cast does not, so ordinary
    lines become loaded. In The Importance of Being Earnest the entire plot runs on
    double identities (Bunburying); in The Tempest, Prospero's whole rule is staged
    spectacle — he arranges a masque of goddesses for Miranda and Ferdinand, then pulls it
    down mid-performance: "We are such stuff / As dreams are made on, and our little life /
    Is rounded with a sleep." The playwright stages a playwright — Prospero's "art" is
    theatre itself, with a tempest as its opening act and forgiveness as its closing
    scene — and every deception on stage quietly says something about the deceptions
    happening in the playhouse around the audience.
  </p>

  <h2>The word-hoard</h2>
  <p>
    The Oxford English Dictionary credits Shakespeare with about 1,700 first recorded uses
    of words: "bedazzled", "swagger", "eyeball", "lonely". The honest footnote matters:
    many were probably in circulation already and he is simply the first surviving
    attestation — but the deeper achievement is <em>capture</em>: familiar words in
    combinations no one had dared. Consider "the multitudinous seas
    incarnadine" or Hamlet's "the undiscovered country from whose bourn no traveller
    returns": ordinary materials, unprecedented pressure. He also pluralised English
    idiom for good — hundreds of phrases still in daily use ("break the ice", "wild-goose
    chase", "in a pickle") are his coinages. When you meet a startling phrase in a set
    speech, do not paraphrase it away: the strangeness is usually the sense.
  </p>

  <h2>Character by contradiction</h2>
  <p>
    Before Shakespeare, drama inherited morality-play types: the Vice, the Hero, the
    Villain. His innovation is characters whose contradiction we recognise as ourselves:
    Hamlet delays and broods; Macbeth knows exactly what he is doing is wrong and does it
    anyway; Shylock is at once antisemitic caricature and the play's most human plea
    ("Hath not a Jew eyes?"). Keats named the capacity in 1817 — <strong>negative
    capability</strong>, the power to hold uncertainties and contradictions without
    irritable reaching after resolution — and it is the reason the plays survive re-staging:
    a character built from a live contradiction re-reads in every era. The Tempest makes
    the point programmatically. Prospero is wronged duke, loving father, and, by any
    modern reading, a colonial master ruling a captive islander whose language he taught
    him — Caliban: "You taught me language, and my profit on't / Is, I know how to curse."
    The play refuses to settle the question, which is why productions from every political
    century keep finding themselves in it.
  </p>

  <h2>Reading the whole text for Paper 2</h2>
  <p>
    Because the drama paper examines one whole set text, build a full-text map of The
    Tempest before you write anything: Act 1 the storm and back-story; Act 2 the
    shipwrecked court's treachery comedy; Act 3 two plots of usurpation (Caliban's and
    Antonio's) converging; Act 4 the masque and its interruption; Act 5 renunciation,
    forgiveness, release — "Every third thought shall be my grave." Then track the
    cross-cutting: Shakespeare runs three plots at different moral temperatures (comic,
    menacing, tender) and intercuts them for contrast, a structural technique straight out
    of the novel lesson. For the comparison essay, the sharpest question across the unit's
    texts is <em>who controls what the audience knows</em> — Priestley's Inspector, Wilde's
    double identities, Prospero's staged illusions and Cleage's and Odimba's intimate
    secrets are all answers to it.
  </p>

  <h2>The pronoun dial: thou and you</h2>
  <p>
    Elizabethan English carried social information modern English lost. <strong>You</strong>
    was the polite, distant form; <strong>thou</strong> was intimate, inferior-addressed or
    insulting — so every pronoun choice is a relationship reading. Watch the dial move:
    a courtier giving a king "you", then "thou" at the moment of betrayal; Juliet to the
    Nurse as intimacy grows; Lear to Goneril — "Thou art a boil" — where the insult is the
    grammar itself. In The Tempest, Prospero addresses Caliban with a mixture of "thou"
    commands and cruel familiarity; Miranda's "Abhorred slave" speech opens with contempt
    and turns on the very next lines to wonder that taught her language. When you annotate
    a set scene, mark every thou/you switch: each one is a visible relationship event,
    worth a sentence of analysis in any essay.
  </p>

  <h2>A soliloquy under the microscope</h2>
  <p>
    Take the most famous speech in English and read it as an argument, because that is
    what it is. "To be, or not to be" opens on the barest possible question — ten
    syllables, built almost entirely of monosyllables, one unstressed "or" in the middle:
    the meter is perfectly regular because the mind is trying to be logical. The speech
    then proceeds like a lawyer's brief: the case for ending it ("to die, to sleep"), the
    counter-case ("perchance to dream — ay, there's the rub"), the evidence (the whips and
    scorns of time, catalogued in parallel), and a verdict that is no verdict — "conscience
    does make cowards of us all", where "conscience" means both morality and thought
    itself. No stage direction says "conflicted"; the syntax, the meter and the failed
    structure of the argument perform it. This is the template for reading any major
    speech: find the question it opens with, map how it argues, and identify where the
    argument breaks — the break is the character.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Textual reality.</strong> We do not have Shakespeare's manuscripts. Roughly
    half the plays appeared in cheap quartos during his lifetime (some good, some badly
    memorial-reconstructed); the First Folio of 1623 — assembled by his colleagues Heminge
    and Condell — printed thirty-six plays and alone preserved about eighteen, The Tempest
    among them (it had never been printed before). Variants between quarto and Folio
    (Hamlet's three texts differ by hundreds of lines) mean every modern edition is a
    scholarly reconstruction, and passages show probable collaboration — Thomas Middleton
    co-wrote Timon of Athens. "Shakespeare" is partly an editorial artefact, which is why
    cruxes (disputed readings) are footnoted in your set text.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The meter evolved.</strong> Counting Shakespeare's lines shows a career-long
    loosening: early plays are tightly end-stopped; the late plays run sentences over line
    breaks, weaken the caesura, and add extra syllables. Stylistic statistics are so
    regular that they are used to date undated plays and to identify collaborators — a
    quantitative vindication of the scansion you already do by ear. And the
    <strong>authorship question</strong> is settled as scholarship: the documentary record
    (contemporary praise, the Folio's testimony of "his papers", business and court
    records) identifies the Stratford man; alternative candidacies are counterfactual fun.
    The interesting mystery is not who wrote the plays but how one career managed the
    reinvention visible in the verse statistics — the answer, as far as scholarship can
    see, is craft, collaborators, and a company that kept him writing for the same actors
    for two decades.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> The First Folio (1623); Kermode, Shakespeare's Language (2000); Taylor &amp; Wells (eds), The Oxford Shakespeare: The Complete Works; the texts of The Tempest, An Inspector Calls, The Importance of Being Earnest, Blues for an Alabama Sky and Princess &amp; The Hustler.</p>
